The '''Balboa Airports Authority''' ({{lang|ca|Autoritat d'Aeroports de Balboa}}; abbreviated BAA) is a [[government agency]] of [[Balboa]] responsible for the ownership, operation, and development of the country's public airports and air traffic control system. It operates under the [[Ministry of Transport (Balboa)|Ministry of Transport]] and manages all 23 public airports in Balboa, including the principal international gateway at [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]] and the secondary hubs at [[Cecilio Ríos International Airport]] and [[Portnou International Airport]]. The authority also provides air traffic control services across Balboan airspace through its air navigation services division.

== History ==

=== Establishment (1973) ===

The Balboa Airports Authority was established in 1973 as part of the administrative reorganisation that followed the [[Treaty of Areza]] and the reunification of Balboa. Prior to the authority's creation, airports in Balboa were owned and operated by a patchwork of municipal governments, provincial administrations, and — in the case of facilities within the former [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|AC mandate territory]] — the [[Adonian Community]] administration. The Treaty of Areza transferred the canal zone and its associated infrastructure to Balboan sovereignty, and the consolidation of all public airports under a single national authority was among the first acts of the new administration.

The authority was placed under the [[Ministry of Transport (Balboa)|Ministry of Transport]] and given responsibility for airport infrastructure, operations, commercial management, capital development, and air traffic control.

=== Sant Cristòfor International Airport (1975–1985) ===

The first major project undertaken by the authority was the planning and construction of [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]], identified in the early 1970s as necessary to replace the constrained existing airport at [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]]. The authority selected the site in [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|Sant Martí]] in 1977, oversaw construction from 1979 to 1984, and opened the airport on 15 March 1985. The new airport became the authority's largest and most significant asset, serving as the hub for the flag carrier [[Aeroístmus]] and the low-cost carrier [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]].

=== Expansion era (1990s–2000s) ===

The authority expanded the national airport network through the 1990s and 2000s. [[Portnou International Airport]] was constructed between 1989 and 1992 to serve the growing city of [[Portnou]] and the canal port complex, opening on 15 June 1992 in time for the [[1994 AFA World Cup]]. [[Badia Curta International Airport]] and [[Haicang International Airport]] were developed as international gateways for the Jala and Estret provincial economies.

At [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]], the authority oversaw the construction of [[Terminal C]] (opened 2003) to accommodate long-haul international traffic and the [[Areza Concourse]] for [[International City of Areza|Areza]]-bound flights. A third runway (06/24) was added in 2008 to separate cargo operations from passenger traffic and increase overall capacity.

== Responsibilities ==

The Balboa Airports Authority is responsible for the following functions across all 23 public airports in Balboa:

* Airport infrastructure: construction, maintenance, and capital development of runways, taxiways, terminals, and associated facilities
* Commercial management: retail concessions, parking, advertising, and property leasing within airport boundaries
* Airport operations: ground handling coordination, gate allocation, terminal management, and passenger services
* Air traffic control: approach and aerodrome control services at all BAA airports, en-route air traffic services across Balboan airspace, and aeronautical information services
* Cargo facilities: cargo aprons, warehousing, and logistics infrastructure at airports with cargo operations
* Safety and security: airport security coordination, emergency services, and compliance with international aviation safety standards
* Essential services: maintaining operations at remote and low-traffic airports serving communities with limited transport alternatives, funded through the authority's cross-subsidy model

The authority's air navigation services division operates control towers at all international airports and major domestic airports, as well as the Balboa Area Control Centre, which provides en-route air traffic control across Balboan airspace.

== Airports ==

{{main|List of airports in Balboa}}

The Balboa Airports Authority owns and operates 23 public airports across Balboa's five provinces, of which 6 are designated as international and 17 serve domestic and regional traffic. The network handled approximately 52.3 million passengers and 660,000 tonnes of cargo in 2025.

== Governance and financing ==

The Balboa Airports Authority is a government agency under the [[Ministry of Transport (Balboa)|Ministry of Transport]]. The authority is headed by a director-general appointed by the Minister of Transport, subject to confirmation by the [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly]]. The director-general serves a five-year term and is responsible for the day-to-day management of the authority, the implementation of capital projects, and the commercial performance of the airport network.

The authority's revenue is derived from airport charges — including landing fees, passenger facility charges, parking, retail concessions, and property leasing — and from air navigation charges, including en-route charges for flights transiting Balboan airspace and approach fees for arrivals and departures at controlled airports. The authority's total revenue for 2024 was approximately $582 million, comprising approximately $399 million in aeronautical revenue (landing fees, passenger facility charges, and aircraft parking), approximately $143 million in non-aeronautical revenue (retail concessions, car parking, and property leasing), and approximately $40 million in air navigation charges. [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]] alone generated approximately $357 million, reflecting its role as the country's principal international gateway and the volume of transit and destination traffic passing through the canal corridor. The authority operates on a cross-subsidy model, using surplus revenue from profitable airports — principally SCI — to fund operations at smaller domestic and regional airports and the air navigation services division, which operates at a net deficit of approximately $30 million annually.

=== Budget ===

The authority's total operating costs for 2024 were approximately $294 million, against total revenue of approximately $582 million, yielding a surplus of approximately $288 million. The surplus is allocated to three purposes:

